// Lesson 3.1 — The Turret: Distance & the Detection Radius
// Problem breakdown: how a thing in the world notices the player.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (read from the teacher's project):
//   Assets/Prefabs/Turret.prefab
//     root  "Turret"            tag Enemy, solid BoxCollider2D, TurretController
//     child "Detection Radius"  CircleCollider2D, m_IsTrigger: 1, m_Radius: 2,
//                               Untagged
//     child "SpawnPoint"        the muzzle, which 3.2 uses
//   Assets/Scripts/TurretController.cs — the built detection:
//         bool inRange = false;  float timer;  float timeBetweenShots = 1.5f;
//         [SerializeField] GameObject fireballPrefab;  [SerializeField] Transform spawnPoint;
//         Transform target;
//         Start(): timer = timeBetweenShots;
//         Update(): if (inRange) { timer -= Time.deltaTime; if (timer < 0) { Shoot(); timer = timeBetweenShots; } }
//         OnTriggerEnter2D: CompareTag("Player") -> inRange = true; target = other.transform;
//         OnTriggerExit2D:  CompareTag("Player") -> inRange = false; target = null;
//
// THE OLD PAGE TAUGHT THE OPPOSITE MECHANISM: Vector3.Distance in Update(),
// a [SerializeField] float range, and a sqrMagnitude comparison. The project
// does none of it — the collider is the radius, and the engine sends the
// enter and exit messages. The distance comparison survives here as the thing
// the collider is doing for you, not as code the student writes.
//
// Scaffolding: OUTLINE ONLY (level 3). The split and the API are given and no
// code is — the student writes TurretController.cs. The ask banner is the level
// marker; a level-3 lesson must not render a walkthrough, and verify-site
// refuses one that does.
//
// Answer-box ids are positional and persisted: the socratic block keeps its
// three questions in order (reworded, never moved) and the decompose block
// keeps its single turn where it was.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// Level 3 (outline only): the split and the API are given, the code is not.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big: 'The turret notices me. That is the whole feature, and "how does a turret notice me" has no answer a computer can act on.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Give the turret a circle it can notice things inside',
      does: 'A `Circle Collider 2D` on a child object, with **Is Trigger** ticked and a radius that reads as "too close" in a playtest. The circle is the range — it is not a number in the script.',
      doc: 'the `Circle Collider 2D` and `Is Trigger` steps from 2.1, and the Turret prefab\'s own `Detection Radius` child.',
      href: '2.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Hold the answer as one true/false field',
      does: '`bool inRange`, in the class body, starting `false`. It is the only thing in the file that remembers anything between frames.',
      doc: 'the class-body field rule from 1.3, and the `bool` you built for `IsWalking` in 1.6.',
      href: '1.3.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Hear the player arrive, and hear them leave',
      does: '`OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)` and `OnTriggerExit2D(Collider2D other)`, each checking `other.gameObject.CompareTag("Player")`. Two callbacks, because a radius has two edges and the player can cross either.',
      doc: 'the `OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)` callback from 2.1, and its exit twin.',
      href: '2.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Remember who walked in',
      does: '`target = other.transform` on the way in, `target = null` on the way out. A reference to the player, so 3.2 knows what to aim at without searching the scene.',
      doc: '`other.transform` in the Unity scripting reference, and the `Transform` you have read since 1.2.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    '"Notice the player" is not something you can write. Each of these four is — and only one is ' +
    'about turrets. A trigger, a bool, two callbacks and a reference are ordinary tools you will ' +
    'reach for all year; the turret is just where they happen to combine.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one ' +
    'search? "Make the turret shoot" cannot be. "Do something when an object enters a trigger" can.',
  turns: [
    'The table below prices two ways to answer "is the player close enough?" — a collider that tells you, and a distance you measure yourself. Say which this project uses and what that choice costs you.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>3.1 — The Turret: Distance &amp; the Detection Radius</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Coins wait to be collected; spikes wait to be bumped into. The turret is the first thing in this
    game that <em>decides</em>. Build <code>TurretController.cs</code> from scratch — and notice which
    half of the job the physics engine does for you.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('in unity how to get the distance between two objects, how to find an object by tag, how to detect when the player is within a radius', 3)}

${renderDecompose('3.1', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> the <strong>Overworld</strong> scene, which holds two
            <strong>Turret</strong> instances. Then <strong>Project window →</strong>
            <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret</code> → <strong>Inspector →</strong> an empty
            <strong>Turret Controller</strong> component is already attached: the script exists, it is
            wired up, and it does nothing — the same situation as <code>PlayerController</code> in
            Unit 1.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → Turret → Detection Radius</strong>. Read the
            <strong>Circle Collider 2D</strong>: <strong>Radius</strong> <code>2</code>,
            <strong>Is Trigger</strong> ticked. That circle <em>is</em> the turret's range. Drag the
            radius in the Scene view while Play is stopped and watch the green ring grow — that ring
            is the whole danger zone.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → Detection Radius → Radius</strong>: give the two turrets in the
            Overworld different values and stand between them. One notices you before the other.
            Nothing in the script changed.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window → Assets/Prefabs/Player → Inspector → Rigidbody 2D</strong>:
            confirm it is there, Dynamic, Gravity Scale <code>0</code>. The body you added in
            <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> is what makes a trigger fire at all — a trigger between two
            objects with no body is a circle that never notices anything.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Prefabs/Player</code>,
            <strong>Inspector → Tag</strong>: confirm the exact spelling your script will ask for, on
            the object carrying the collider. A tag that does not match is not an error, it is
            silence.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Once the code half is done — Game view → press Play</strong> and walk slowly
            toward a turret: quiet outside the ring, a line in the Console the moment you cross it,
            quiet again as you walk back out.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        There are two ways to answer <em>"is the player close enough?"</em>, and they are not the
        same amount of work. This project takes the first row.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Approach</th><th>Who does the measuring</th><th>What you get</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td>A trigger collider on a child, plus <code>OnTriggerEnter2D()</code> / <code>OnTriggerExit2D()</code></td>
            <td><strong>The physics engine.</strong> It tests shapes against shapes every step and sends you a message when the answer changes.</td>
            <td>An <em>event</em> on the way in and another on the way out, and a radius a designer drags in the Scene view.</td>
            <td>Nothing fires when an object appears inside the circle without crossing its edge; and the circle needs a body on one of the two objects to work at all.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Vector3.Distance(a, b)</code> in <code>Update()</code>, compared against a <code>range</code> field</td>
            <td><strong>You do</strong>, sixty times a second, whether anything changed or not.</td>
            <td>A real distance in world units — printable, and useful if you need <em>how far</em>.</td>
            <td>A square root every frame, a target reference you have to find yourself, and no event: you re-derive the same true or false forever.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>[SerializeField] float range</code></td>
            <td>You, by typing a number.</td>
            <td>A tunable radius per instance.</td>
            <td>It is invisible: you cannot see it in the Scene view, so "the player was inside" and "the player was not" look identical until you print something.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>other.gameObject.CompareTag("Player")</code></td>
            <td>The engine, on a tag comparison.</td>
            <td>Only the player counts — a coin rolling through the ring does not trip the turret.</td>
            <td>A tag is a promise nothing enforces: anything tagged <code>Player</code> will trip every turret in the scene.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification for <code>TurretController.cs</code>:</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which object owns the circle, and why is it a <em>child</em> rather than the turret's own collider? What would happen if the turret's solid body were also the trigger?</li>
            <li>What single field remembers that the player is inside, what type is it, and what value must it start at?</li>
            <li>Which two callbacks does the radius produce, and what does each one set? Name the field each callback writes, and say what would break if only one existed.</li>
            <li>What is stored in <code>target</code>, and why store it at all when <code>Update()</code> could look the player up?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Choosing a Targeting Approach</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>The trigger knows nothing about distance — it fires when two shapes overlap. What does the collider do for you that a <code>Vector3.Distance(a, b)</code> call in <code>Update()</code> would make you do by hand, every frame?</li>
            <li><code>inRange</code> is set true on the way in and false on the way out. Name a moment when the player could be inside the circle and your field would still say otherwise.</li>
            <li>You have two turrets in this scene and one player. Each turret keeps its own <code>target</code>. What would go wrong if that field were <code>static</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Break "the turret notices you" into a shape, a remembered answer, two callbacks and a reference</li>
        <li>Say why the radius is a collider on a child rather than a number in the script</li>
        <li>Explain why a trigger needs a Rigidbody2D on one of the two objects to fire at all</li>
        <li>Name one thing the trigger model cannot tell you, and what you would have to add to know it</li>
        <li>Say what <code>target</code> is for, and who will read it in 3.2</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

